WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.260
String risk often increases at boundaries with C libraries, operating system APIs, plugins, foreign function interfaces, older code, and platform-specific encodings.

2
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:18.120
These boundaries may not share modern C++ assumptions about ownership, length, termination, mutability, or encoding.

3
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.920
A legacy API may expect a null-terminated string.

4
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.940
Another may expect a mutable caller-provided buffer and a length.

5
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:30.440
Another may return a pointer that remains valid only until the next call.

6
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:34.540
Wide string and narrow string conventions can differ by platform.

7
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:42.640
The dangerous part is not the existence of these APIs; it is letting their conventions spread through the application without a clear boundary.

8
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.080
Wrappers can isolate these details.

9
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:56.140
A small wrapper can validate lengths, handle conversion errors, define ownership, translate platform-specific return values, and expose safer application-level types.

10
00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:01.920
The rest of the code can then operate on clear C++ objects instead of pointer-only contracts.

11
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.920
Interop code should be strict about failure.

12
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:11.980
Conversion errors, truncation, missing terminators, and unexpected output lengths should be handled deliberately.

13
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.600
If the application cannot represent the result safely, it should fail clearly rather than guessing.

14
00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.920
Defensive design also keeps review focused.

15
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:29.180
When risky string conventions are concentrated at a boundary, engineers know where to look for length discipline, encoding rules, and lifetime assumptions.
